    "title": "The Nota Simple sizes the mortgage",
    "source_slug": "nota_simple_en",
    "source_slug2": "land_registry_en",
    "source_article": "nota-simple.txt, land-registry.txt",
    "post_text": "The cheapest document in a Spanish purchase can quietly shrink your buyer's mortgage. Banks read the Nota Simple more carefully than buyers do.\n\nIf the cadastral value on it sits 10k under the price, the bank may lend 10k less. That's the detail from the Land Registry that catches foreign buyers off guard, and it's exactly the kind of thing an agent can flag early.\n\nThe document itself costs around 10 euros online and can arrive the same day, or under 4 euros in person at the registry, where it's usually ready within a few days. You supply the property details or its identifier, the IDUFIR, which the seller or the agent usually has to hand. Anyone with a legitimate interest in buying can request one, and even a friend or family member can order it on the buyer's behalf, since the registrar decides whether the interest is legitimate. If your client needs it translated, that's a further fee worth mentioning upfront.\n\nIt shows the whole picture. The registered owner, when they bought and with which notary they signed, the property description and square metres, boundaries, the land classification, and any charges. Mortgages, unpaid taxes, debts. In Spain those debts sit on the property, so your buyer inherits whatever that page reveals. It also settles quieter questions: that the seller really is the owner, because there have been cases of properties sold by people with no right to sell them, and that no inheritance dispute is hanging over the title.\n\nWhich is why two moments matter. Before the arras, because any debt or discrepancy is far easier to resolve before 10 percent changes hands. And at mortgage time, because when the registered cadastral value comes in below the offer, the bank looks at that lower figure, not the price your buyer agreed.\n\nIf the page shows something unexpected, the next call is a property lawyer, not the seller.\n\nOrder it for every serious buyer before the arras is drafted. Ten euros of paper beats six weeks of surprises.\n\n#NotaSimple #BuyingInSpain #RealEstateSpain",
    "image_url": null
