    "title": "The owner's first twelve months",
    "post_text": "Your client's first year of Spanish ownership has a calendar. Nobody hands it to them at the notary, so it tends to arrive one panicked message at a time.\n\nHere's the rough shape of it. Deadlines shift between municipalities and autonomous communities, so treat this as a planning framework and check the local ayuntamiento before quoting a date.\n\nJanuary is tax and review. Rental income declared annually gets filed for the previous year, and it's the sensible moment to check that last year's utilities, community fees, insurance and tax all actually went out.\n\nFebruary is the rental registration question. The NRA, the Número de Registro de Alquiler, is currently on hold after the Supreme Court declared it illegal, so the honest answer to clients is paused, not gone.\n\nMarch is post winter. Plumbing, damp, appliances, heating and water systems, especially on the coast or in a home that sat empty for months. Small fixes now instead of expensive ones in July. It's also when photos and listings get refreshed for the season ahead.\n\nApril is utility contracts. Tariffs move, and plenty of non resident owners sit on an outdated one for years simply because nobody rang round.\n\nMay is summer prep. Air conditioning serviced, pool started, garden cleared, cleaning schedules and key handover confirmed. June is the insurance review, worth doing because Spanish policies renew annually, so there's one window a year to get a competing quote.\n\nJuly brings community fees, often quarterly, often by direct debit, though communities differ wildly and plenty bill in January or monthly. August is quiet, since Spanish offices slow right down or close.\n\nSeptember is post season repairs and checking rental platforms took the right commission. October is the heavy one. IBI usually lands around then on the Costa Blanca, rubbish tax often runs from late July to mid October, and on the Costa del Sol IBI is often billed twice, spring and autumn.\n\nNovember is the banking review, hidden costs and exchange rates. December is imputed income tax, the Modelo 210, usually due by 31 December when it's paid annually.\n\nNone of that is your job, and all of it still reaches you, months after the commission cleared. It's the year Bueno covers for the owner, Modelo 210 filing and property admin included, with human support in their own language. Happy to walk you through how the handover works.\n\n#RealEstateSpain #Proptech #SpainProperty",
