    "title": "The Modelo 210 answer on the spot",
    "source_slug": "modelo210_scenarios_en",
    "source_slug2": "modelo210_en",
    "source_article": "non-resident-property-tax.txt, irnr.txt",
    "post_text": "Every foreign buyer eventually asks what the home will cost them in tax each year. Sketch the Modelo 210 answer on the spot and you've earned their trust instantly.\n\nEvery non resident owner files it, even when the property earns nothing, and the answer is small enough to memorise.\n\nThe formula runs cadastral value times 2 percent, or 1.1 percent where the municipality has had a general cadastral revision in the previous 10 tax years, times 19 percent for EU and EEA residents or 24 percent for everyone else, times days owned over 365.\n\nThe numbers calm people down. A UK owner with a cadastral value of 45,986 euros pays around 220 euros for a full year. That's it.\n\nNow the scenarios worth carrying. Buy mid year without renting, and the first return is due the following year, paid by year end, covering only the days of ownership. Sell, and the owner files for the days up to the sale, early the next year. Rent it out and the rhythm changes, because rental income is declared quarterly, by the 20th of April, July, October and January, with the annual return still covering any empty days. Booking platforms now report revenue to the Spanish tax office. And co-owners each file their own share, so two names on the deed means two returns.\n\nThe penalty ladder rewards honesty, so quote it precisely. File voluntarily before the tax office makes contact and penalties can be avoided entirely. Pay between 1 and 12 months late and the surcharge runs 1 to 12 percent of the tax owed. Beyond 12 months it's 15 percent plus interest. But if the tax office reaches the owner first, the fine can reach at least 50 percent of the tax owed.\n\nOne nuance for landlord clients: straightforward holiday lets are exempt from VAT, but hotel style services like meals or regular cleaning bring 10 percent VAT, and a sublease contract with a broker or platform makes the rental commercial at 21.\n\nPull the cadastral value from the IBI bill and run the numbers with your buyer.\n\n#Modelo210 #SpanishTax #RealEstateSpain",
