    "title": "What the holiday home costs a year",
    "post_text": "Buyers ask what the place costs to run and usually get a shrug. There's a better answer, and it's more useful than any yield conversation.\n\nFor a typical apartment of 50 to 100 square metres, based on what owners actually pay, the monthly picture looks roughly like this.\n\nCommunity fees around 50 euros, with large variations between communities. Electricity around 100. Gas around 25, and where there's no gas the electricity figure goes up instead. Water around 25. Internet around 30. Insurance around 20. IBI around 50. A renovation reserve of about 10. National tax around 15. A Spanish payment account, roughly 8 a month.\n\nThat comes to about 333 euros a month as a simple estimate.\n\nThen the two extras most buyers forget. A company to look after the property while they're not there adds 50 to 100 euros a month. An alarm, which is worth having on a home that stands empty, is about another 50. So a realistic figure for an apartment is 400 to 500 euros a month, not the 333.\n\nFor a detached house or villa in active use through the year, double that or more. Electricity alone can run 300 to 500 euros a month in summer if the air conditioning is working hard, and a pool pushes the water bill up substantially.\n\nThe value of saying this out loud at viewing stage is that it changes the conversation. A buyer who's budgeted 500 a month doesn't get a nasty surprise in year one, doesn't fall behind on IBI, and doesn't come back to you in March asking why the bills look like this.\n\nThat running cost list is the part of ownership that lands on the agent afterwards, question by question, unpaid. Bueno is one subscription at 99 euros a year covering the Spanish account, energy switching, Modelo 210 filing, insurance and multilingual support, which is what sits behind that 8 a month line. Give me a shout if you'd like the detail.\n\n#RealEstateSpain #Proptech #SpainProperty",
